If the bottleneck pathology identified in \cref{lem:rare-bottleneck} is relevant to manipulation, then \name{}'s representation should produce improvements in performance on tasks requiring precise object interactions.
We test this across 11 tasks from OGBench, MetaWorld, and ManiSkill (\cref{tab:manipulation}); dataset details are provided in \cref{app:data-mw-ms}.
Across all three benchmarks, \name{} consistently improves over FB, with average gains of $35.5$, $43.6$, and $29.7$ percentage points on OGBench, MetaWorld, and ManiSkill, respectively, and outperforms both HILP and RLDP on every task.
On OGBench, where goal-conditioned baselines are also available, \name{} exceeds GCIQL on \texttt{cube-single} and \texttt{scene}, while GCIQL performs better on \texttt{cube-double}.
This comparison is particularly stringent because GCIQL, CRL, and QRL are trained explicitly for goal reaching, whereas \name{} learns a task-independent successor representation and receives the downstream reward only through $10^4$ reward-labeled offline transitions used for post-hoc task inference.
